;; THE OPERATOR MANUAL — the harness's own command surface, as data.
;;
;; The agent develops at the REPL inside this image, so the functions it can
;; usefully call are part of its tool surface whether or not anything documents
;; them. Without a curated list the only way to find them is to guess a
;; namespace and read `complete` output, which is how a whole capability stays
;; invisible.
;;
;; llm-repl solves this by tagging vars with ^{:manual "sentence"} and
;; compiling the manual from ns-publics. That is the right shape and the wrong
;; PLACE for us: metadata lives in src, and src is the half of this system the
;; supervisor cannot edit at runtime. So the curation lives here instead —
;; which entries exist, and the one-sentence summary each gets. The mechanism
;; that resolves and renders it is samizdat.manual.
;;
;; TWO AUDIENCES, TWO TEXTS, ONE SEAM. The docstring on the var stays
;; maintainer-dense; :summary is the operator sentence. `samizdat.manual/render`
;; shows the summaries, `entries` returns both as data, and anything that wants
;; the full docstring reads :doc off the resolved var.
;;
;; Every :name must resolve. A name that does not is a loud failure, not a hole
;; in the rendering: a manual that quietly lists nothing is worse than no
;; manual, because it reads as "there is nothing here".
;;
;; Adding an entry needs no rebuild. Removing one hides a capability, so remove
;; only what has actually gone away.
