 {:group "Bounded verification"
  :entries
  [{:name samizdat.security.verification-env/run
    :summary "The M2 VerificationEnvironment: bounded done's tests run inside a controller-owned, fail-closed bubblewrap sandbox over a private copy of the root — never a direct host spawn."}
   {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/available?
    :summary "Whether this host can run that sandbox (Linux + bwrap + prlimit + user namespaces). When false, bounded done refuses outright rather than degrade."}
   {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/focused-argv
    :summary "The bounded lane's verifier argv: the controller-PINNED executable and fixed args (code, not gates.edn) plus the one derived focused -e expression."}
    {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/coordinate
     :summary "A stable digest naming the pinned sandbox policy itself — the value the journal's ship-verify row carries as :verify-env."}
    {:name samizdat.security.canonical-edn/coordinate
     :summary "This repository's independent keeper of the canonical EDN grammar (RFC-012): sha256 over the tagged tree of [:bb4t.coordinate/v1 kind tree], domain-separated by kind. Cross-pinned against the other ecosystem's keeper by shared golden vectors — a moved digest means the contract drifted."}
    {:name samizdat.security.canonical-edn/canonical-tree
     :summary "The inert EDN domain as an ordered tagged tree — the form both keepers digest. Rejects anything alive or ambiguous (metadata, records, floats, lazy seqs, objects)."}
    {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/describe-envelope
     :summary "What the sandbox IS, as the execution environment SPI's describe envelope: the inert shape-only description beside its recomputed coordinate — the name a second repository can check (RFC-012)."}
    {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/verify-envelope
     :summary "One run projected into the SPI's run envelope — attribution, input coordinate, invocation index, status, streams, duration — exactly what the ship gate journals beside its verdict; nil when nothing spawned."}
    {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/workspace-manifest
     :summary "The manifest of a workspace as the verifier's private copy of it: entries sorted by path, files with byte size and content digest, links as links, exclusions applied by name and recorded."}
    {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/input-coordinate
     :summary "The canonical-EDN coordinate naming exactly what one verification ran against — the PRIVATE COPY, taken after staging and before the spawn so it names the input the run started from (RFC-012)."}
    {:name samizdat.security.verification-env/invocation-count
     :summary "How many sandboxed verifications this process's environment attempted. Real spawns only — probes, refusals and staging failures never move it; a future replay must not either."}
    {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-verification-env/run
     :summary "The SmolVM VerificationEnvironment — the bounded done's second controller-owned verify provider, ported from bbagent's measured execution substrate: every run boots an ephemeral machine whose only host filesystem is the project mounted read-only, overlays a writable layer inside the VM, hides the manifest's excluded paths, drops to the project owner's identity with no capabilities, and runs the pinned IN-IMAGE verifier argv. The project is bracketed by its input coordinate; a project that moved under a run is :project-changed and claims no coordinate."}
    {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-verification-env/available?
     :summary "Whether this host can run that environment (Linux + the measured smolvm manager version + the configured guest image + a real minimum machine spawn). When false, bounded done refuses outright rather than degrade."}
    {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-verification-env/guest-image
     :summary "The configured guest image archive (SAMIZDAT_SMOLVM_IMAGE, digest-pinned by SAMIZDAT_SMOLVM_IMAGE_SHA256) and its digest — trusted-controller environment, never gates.edn, never model-facing. The digest participates in the environment's coordinate, so a different image is a different environment."}
    {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-verification-env/focused-argv
     :summary "This environment's verifier argv: the pinned IN-IMAGE toolchain (babashka — its identity is the image digest, never a host path) and fixed args plus the one derived focused -e expression shared with every other lane."}
    {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-verification-env/input-manifest
     :summary "The project input manifest and its coordinate — the live-tree bracket. The same grammar and kind the bwrap environment's private-copy coordinate uses, so both environments name one input the same way; absolute or escaping symlinks are refused (the machine cannot be handed them faithfully) and the excluded paths the walk refused are exactly the paths the prelude hides from the workload."}
    {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-verification-env/verify-envelope
     :summary "One run projected into the SPI's run envelope — including the :project-changed status the bwrap environment cannot produce: an unanchored run carries no input coordinate and its process outcome is demoted to :output/process."}
    {:name samizdat.security.verification-provider/selected
    :summary "Trusted controller selection between the bounded lane's verify environments (:bwrap default, :smolvm via SAMIZDAT_VERIFY_ENV) — code and the harness process's own environment, never gates.edn. Fail closed both ways: an unavailable selected provider refuses with its own reason, and an unrecognized name refuses as :unknown-provider rather than quietly running the default."}
    {:name samizdat.security.closure-coverage/signature
     :summary "The ClosureCoverageSignature: what a closure verification actually covered, read out of its own summary in either toolchain dialect, beside the suite/verifier/input coordinates that make a count mean something. A closure result whose summary cannot be read, that reports zero tests, or that contradicts its own verdict is refused as evidence; a coverage DECREASE only warns."}
    {:name samizdat.security.closure-coverage/delta
     :summary "The coverage movement from a clean-target baseline to a final closure signature. Exposes the numbers and whether they fell; decides nothing, and never compares mechanically across two different suites."}]}

 {:group "Model-authorized project execution"
  :entries
  [{:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/run
    :summary "The JS2 ProjectExecutionEnvironment — where a MODEL's own argv runs. An ephemeral machine per execution whose only host filesystem is the project mounted read-only and then masked; everything the workload writes lands in an overlay that dies with the machine, so the authoritative tree is byte-identical afterwards and project/edit remains the only thing that changes it. No network, no host environment, no capabilities, unprivileged, bounded streams and wall clock. Its result is DEVELOPMENT evidence and settles nothing about completion."}
   {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/validate-request
    :summary "One model request as a validated execution request: argv as a non-empty vector of bounded non-blank strings (never a shell line), and a CLOSED option set of :cwd and :timeout-ms only. The image, network, mounts, environment, resource limits, identity, cleanup and provider are controller constants and are refused BY NAME rather than ignored; the timeout may narrow the pinned ceiling and never widen it."}
   {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/environment-description
    :summary "What this environment IS, in the RFC-012 vocabulary — :executor/mode :project-run, operations #{:describe :run}, :writeback :none, and the argv named as :model-supplied. The mode is what keeps a development envelope from being read as an acceptance one."}
   {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/run-envelope
    :summary "One execution projected into the SPI's run envelope, with the invocation index, the attribution, the staged input coordinate, the bounded streams and the disposition. Development evidence, journalled and compared under keys the verify envelopes cannot be confused with."}
   {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/invocation-count
    :summary "How many real machine executions this process's environment attempted. Process-local by construction, and moved by nothing but a spawn — which is what makes 'a replayed project/run launched nothing' a checkable claim."}
   {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/poisoned?
    :summary "Whether a timed-out execution left this environment unusable. A host deadline bounds host WAITING and does not prove the child inside the machine died, so a timeout poisons, the machines this invocation OWNS are stopped and deleted, the manager's table is re-asked, and only a clean answer lifts the poison."}
   {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/owned-machines
    :summary "Which machines one execution may clean up: exactly the machine the manager's own banner named for its spawn, and otherwise NOTHING. Ownership has one source, because every inference from the manager's table is wrong under concurrency — the single machine that appeared since a baseline can be another run's, and that is precisely the case an inference is most confident about. The table travels on as evidence (:cleanup/candidates), never as authority; unknown ownership deletes nothing, is never clean, and leaves the invocation poisoned."}
   {:name samizdat.security.smolvm-project-env/unresolved-poison
    :summary "The invocations whose timeout cleanup has not been shown clean, with the evidence each carries. A SET, because one slot cannot hold two overlapping timeouts: the second overwrote the first and whichever cleaned up first cleared the other's uncertainty too. Each invocation poisons and resolves its own entry; a new execution is refused while any remains; no invocation can clear another's."}
   {:name samizdat.security.project-execution-provider/selected
    :summary "Trusted controller selection of the project execution environment (:smolvm default, via SAMIZDAT_PROJECT_EXEC_ENV) — code and the harness process's own environment, never gates.edn. Deliberately separate from the VERIFY provider selector: shared mechanism, separate authority. Fail closed both ways, and never a fallback to a host process, a shell, or the verify sandbox."}]}

 {:group "Reading and writing Clojure"
  :entries
  [{:name samizdat.lisp/diagnose
    :summary "Why a piece of Clojure would not load AS WRITTEN, or nil — reason plus 1-based line/col, naming both ends of a mismatch. Answers 'is this loadable now', which is a different question from lisp/balance's 'could this be repaired': the delimiters balancing does not promise it READS, and `(defn f [] :)` is perfectly balanced and is not Clojure."}
   {:name samizdat.lisp/balance
    :summary "The repair half: closes a trailing truncation or trims a trailing over-close when the result reads, and refuses anything mid-file because closing it would re-parent code. Returns data, not sentences — the words live in prompts/file-tool.md."}
   {:name samizdat.lisp/first-odd-quote-line
    :summary "The line where the running unescaped-quote count first goes odd — the line that most likely opened a string nobody closed, which is NOT where the reader complains."}
   {:name samizdat.hashline/line-anchor
    :summary "Mint a `<line>:<hash>` anchor: the line locates, the three-hex content hash of the trimmed line verifies. What read_file({anchors: true}) prints and the patch tool spends."}
   {:name samizdat.hashline/apply-edits
    :summary "Apply a batch of anchored edits atomically. Every edit resolves against the same read, so order does not matter; one stale anchor or two edits over a line refuses the whole batch rather than half-writing it."}
   {:name samizdat.hashline/resolve-one
    :summary "The refusal ladder behind an anchor: exact, drifted-so-follow-the-content, ambiguous-hash-so-the-explicit-line-wins, far-away-so-REFUSE, and gone-but-here-is-the-current-anchor. A false refuse costs one re-read; a false accept corrupts the file."}]}

